# Template for the workspace-root provisioning credential file (gitignored):
#
#   .env.provisioning   the cluster's provisioning account, used for one-off
#                       provisioning only — creating roles, databases,
#                       extensions and grants. Nothing in this repo reads it at
#                       runtime; it is sourced by hand for psql runs.
#
# It lives at the workspace root, beside .env.test, because both are
# cluster-and-machine state rather than instance state. An instance's own
# runtime credential belongs under that instance (e.g.
# instances/policy_db/.env), never here.
#
# The account is NOT necessarily a superuser, and the filename no longer
# implies one — on some machines it holds CREATEDB + CREATEROLE with
# rolsuper = f. What it must be able to do (MAINTAINING.packages.md §2):
#
#   (a) create a role                        CREATEROLE or SUPERUSER
#   (b) create a database owned by that role CREATEDB or SUPERUSER, plus
#                                            SET ROLE to the owner
#   (c) install ltree and vector             a superuser, or a cluster whose
#                                            template1 was already seeded
#
# create_ingestion_test_db.sql checks each of these before relying on it and
# stops with the exact fixing command when one is missing. One step needs a
# true superuser whatever this account is: instances/policy_db/sql/
# mcp_ro_policy_grants.sql, for the reasons MAINTAINING.instance.policy_db.md
# §3 gives.
#
# Populate it BY HAND. Nothing generates it, and no script writes a password
# into any file.
#
# Same permissions rule as every credential file — no other local account may
# be able to read it:
#
#   chmod 600 .env.provisioning     # macOS/Linux; re-apply by hand (umask 022)
#   icacls .env.provisioning        # Windows: owner + administrators only,
#                                   # no Users or Everyone entry
#
# All four variables are required.
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost
POSTGRES_PORT=5432
POSTGRES_USER=<the cluster's provisioning role>
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<that role's password>
